# --allow-egress kernel-config delta: expose a fourth 8250 UART (COM4 / ttyS3).
#
# Human-readable RECORD of the change (the authoritative config is
# microvm-kernel-x86_64-6.1.config, which build_kernel.sh copies to .config and
# runs through olddefconfig -- that strips comments, hence this file, matching the
# test1a-com2.config convention).
#
# Two-line change vs the TEST-1a config:
CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_NR_UARTS=4
CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_RUNTIME_UARTS=4
#
# Rationale:
#   --allow-egress adds a host-mediated egress transport: a fourth 16550 (COM4,
#   port 0x2e8, ISA IRQ3) in the VMM, driven by a guest-side forwarder that
#   opens /dev/ttyS3. TEST-1a raised CONFIG_SERIAL_8250_NR_UARTS to 2 (ttyS0
#   console + ttyS1 control). The 8250 driver HARD-CAPS the registered-port count
#   at NR_UARTS -- the `8250.nr_uarts=` module param is clamped to it -- so ttyS3
#   could never appear at runtime without a rebuild. Raising both the compile-time
#   cap (NR_UARTS) and the default count (RUNTIME_UARTS) to 4 makes the kernel
#   probe + register the standard COM3/COM4 legacy ports; COM4 (0x2e8) responds so
#   /dev/ttyS3 exists. COM3 (ttyS2, 0x3e8, IRQ4) is intentionally left unbacked --
#   NR_UARTS=4 is the smallest cap that includes the COM4 slot; the open COM3 bus
#   is harmless (autoconfig finds no UART -> port stays PORT_UNKNOWN).
#
#   IRQ3 is SHARED with COM2 (ttyS1): both are identity-routed to IO-APIC pin 3 by
#   the MP table (src/mptable.rs), so no interrupt-routing change is needed. Linux
#   registers IRQ3 with IRQF_SHARED and its 8250 handler polls every port on the
#   line, so the shared edge is serviced for whichever UART asserted it.
#
# Reproducibility: the rebuilt vmlinux is a NEW pinned artifact; regenerate the
# reproducible build + digests with `tdvmm build-kernel --record` after applying
# these two values to microvm-kernel-x86_64-6.1.config, then re-run
# scripts/gen_manifest.sh. The reproducibility unit stays the (vmlinux sha256 +
# initramfs sha256) pair.
